Prerequisites                                                                                      
                                                                                                   
  docker compose --env-file config/.env down -v   # clean slate                                      
  make up-full                                      # or just: make up                             
                                                                                                     
  Wait for all services to be healthy, then open:
  - Admin UI: http://localhost:5173                                                                  
  - Admin API: http://localhost:8086                                                               

  Login: use master key $LITELLM_KEY

  ---
  Phase 1: Foundation

  1. Organizations

  - Navigate to Organizations in sidebar
  - Click Create Organization → name: "Acme Corp", slug: "acme-corp"
  - Click into it → you'll see tabs: Overview, Business Units, Teams, Members, SSO
  - Create a Business Unit: name "Engineering", slug "engineering"
  - Assign a team (if any exist from LiteLLM seed data)

  2. SSO Config

  - In the org detail → SSO tab
  - Configure an OIDC provider (provider_type: "oidc", provider_name: "okta", client_id:
  "test-client")
  - This is a stub — it stores config but doesn't do real OIDC yet

  3. Audit Log

  - Navigate to Audit Log
  - You should already see entries from the org/BU you just created
  - Try the filters (resource_type, action)
  - Click Export CSV and Export JSON

  4. Guardrails + DLP (extends existing)

  - Navigate to Guardrails → create a guardrail config
  - API test for DLP detectors:
  curl -s http://localhost:8086/api/v1/detectors \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer $(curl -s http://localhost:8086/auth/login -d
  '{"api_key":"$LITELLM_KEY"}' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' | jq -r
  .access_token)" | jq

  ---
  Phase 2: Governance

  5. Prompts

  - Navigate to Prompts
  - Create a template: name "Summarize", slug "summarize", template text: Summarize the following:
  {{text}}
  - View it in the list, click to see details
  - Submit for review → check the approvals section → approve it

  6. Rate Limits

  - Navigate to Rate Limits
  - Create a policy: name "Default User RPM", scope "user", rpm_limit: 60
  - Check the events table (will be empty until limits are hit)

  7. Model Access

  - Navigate to Model Access
  - Tiers tab: you should see 3 seeded tiers (standard, premium, experimental)
  - Create a new tier if you like
  - Requests tab: submit an access request for a model
  - Approve/reject it from the admin view

  ---
  Phase 3: FinOps

  8. Chargeback

  - Navigate to Chargeback
  - Allocation Rules tab: create a rule (name: "Eng Team", allocation_type: "cost_center",
  allocation_target: "CC-1001")
  - Reports tab: click Generate Report for current month (e.g. "2026-02")
  - Export as CSV
  - Forecasts tab: click Generate Forecast (needs some spend data to be meaningful)

  9. SLA Monitor

  - Navigate to SLA Monitor
  - Provider Health tab: shows cards per provider (may be empty without traffic)
  - Create an SLA definition: name "GPT-4o SLA", target_p95_ms: 5000, target_error_rate: 0.01
  - Failover Rules tab: create a rule (primary: "gpt-4o", fallback: "claude-sonnet-4-5")

  ---
  Phase 4: Intelligence

  10. A/B Tests

  - Navigate to A/B Tests
  - Create a test: name "GPT vs Claude", base_model: "gpt-4o", variant_model: "claude-sonnet-4-5",
  traffic_split: 20
  - Click Start → status should change to "running"
  - Click into the test to see detail view with snapshot history
  - Click Stop or Promote

  11. Semantic Cache (API only — no dedicated page)

  # Get a token first
  TOKEN=$(curl -s http://localhost:8086/auth/login \
    -d '{"api_key":"$LITELLM_KEY"}' \
    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' | jq -r .access_token)

  # Check cache stats
  curl -s http://localhost:8086/api/v1/cache/stats \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" | jq

  # List cache entries
  curl -s http://localhost:8086/api/v1/cache/entries \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" | jq

  ---
  Phase 5: Developer Experience

  12. Events

  - Navigate to Events
  - Subscriptions tab: create a subscription (name: "Slack Alerts", channel: "webhook", event_types:
  ["budget.exceeded", "guardrail.blocked"], config: {"url": "https://hooks.slack.com/test"})
  - Event Log tab: click Send Test Event → pick an event type, enter a JSON payload
  - Event should appear in the log table

  13. Playground Sessions (API only)

  # Save a session
  curl -s http://localhost:8086/api/v1/playground/sessions \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -d '{"name":"Test Session","prompt":"Hello
  world","models":["gpt-4o","claude-sonnet-4-5"],"is_public":true}' | jq

  # List sessions
  curl -s http://localhost:8086/api/v1/playground/sessions \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" | jq

  14. Model Deprecations (API only)

  # Create a deprecation notice
  curl -s http://localhost:8086/api/v1/model-deprecations \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -d '{"model_name":"gpt-4","replacement_model":"gpt-4o","deprecation_date":"2026-03-01","sunset_da
  te":"2026-06-01","message":"Please migrate to gpt-4o"}' | jq

  # Check if a model is deprecated
  curl -s http://localhost:8086/api/v1/model-deprecations/check/gpt-4 \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" | jq

  # Check a non-deprecated model
  curl -s http://localhost:8086/api/v1/model-deprecations/check/gpt-4o \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" | jq

